
2. JavaScript Data Types: Notes and Methods
a. String Data Type
Strings are used to represent and manipulate a sequence of characters. They are written inside single quotes ('), double quotes ("), or backticks (`).

3 Common Methods:

    .toUpperCase(): Converts the entire string to uppercase letters.

    .includes(substring): Checks if a string contains a specific sequence of characters, returning true or false.

    .slice(start, end): Extracts a section of a string and returns it as a new string without modifying the original.

    b. Number Data Type
The Number data type represents both integer and floating-point values (decimals). Unlike some other languages, JavaScript doesn't have separate types for integers and doubles.

3 Common Methods:

    .toFixed(n): Formats a number with exactly n digits after the decimal point (returns a string).

.toString(): Converts a number into a string.

Number.isInteger(value): A static method that determines whether the passed value is an integer.